{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/master/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Trstprep Security Audit",
          "version": "2.1.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/cloudflare/security-audit-skill",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-01-CSRF-COOKIE-BYPASS",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_01_CSRF_COOKIE_BYPASS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Anti-CSRF Token Validation Neutralization via Ambient Cookie Ingestion"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend anti-CSRF token verification middleware extracts candidate CSRF tokens from req.cookies._csrf_token when headers are absent. Because cookies are ambient credentials automatically attached by browsers during cross-site requests, any forged request satisfies the csrfTokensEqual validation check identically to an authentic user request, completely disabling CSRF protection across all cookie-authenticated endpoints."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend anti-CSRF token verification middleware extracts candidate CSRF tokens from req.cookies._csrf_token when headers are absent. Because cookies are ambient credentials automatically attached by browsers during cross-site requests, any forged request satisfies the csrfTokensEqual validation check identically to an authentic user request, completely disabling CSRF protection across all cookie-authenticated endpoints.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/backend/src/middleware/csrf.middleware.js:437, the csrfToken candidate resolution chain includes req.cookies?._csrf_token as a valid input source. Browsers automatically transmit cookies on cross-origin requests unless SameSite=strict is enforced. Combined with production auth settings allowing SameSite=none (auth.service.js:25) or same-site subdomain traversals, an attacker's cross-site request supplies both the session token and the valid CSRF token simultaneously.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nRemove req.cookies?._csrf_token from candidate token resolution in csrf.middleware.js. Require all CSRF tokens to arrive via custom request headers (X-CSRF-Token, X-XSRF-Token) or body parameters."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "critical",
                "likelihood": "high",
                "impact": "critical",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-02-CSRF-TEST-ENGINE-OMISSION",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_02_CSRF_TEST_ENGINE_OMISSION",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Complete Omission of CSRF Protection on Testing Engine & Session Revocation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The core testing engine routers (/api/tests, /api/attempt, /api/questions) and user session management router (/api/sessions) omit the validateCsrfToken middleware entirely. Because authentication accepts session cookies via protect, an attacker can submit, tamper with, or prematurely terminate active examination attempts or revoke user sessions without authorization."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The core testing engine routers (/api/tests, /api/attempt, /api/questions) and user session management router (/api/sessions) omit the validateCsrfToken middleware entirely. Because authentication accepts session cookies via protect, an attacker can submit, tamper with, or prematurely terminate active examination attempts or revoke user sessions without authorization.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/backend/src/app-port5001.js lines 1105, 1129, and 1133, testsRoutes, questionsRoutes, attemptRoutes, and sessionRoutes are registered without mounting validateCsrfToken. Although protect verifies JWT tokens, it extracts them from cookies (req.cookies.token), leaving mutations exposed to CSRF.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nMount validateCsrfToken on testsRoutes, questionsRoutes, attemptRoutes, and sessionRoutes in apps/backend/src/app-port5001.js."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "critical",
                "likelihood": "high",
                "impact": "critical",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-03-OUT-OF-PIPELINE-ADMIN-ROUTES",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_03_OUT_OF_PIPELINE_ADMIN_ROUTES",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Out-of-Pipeline Admin Routes Bypassing Origin, API Key, RBAC & Audit Middleware"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Several administrative routers are mounted directly on top-level paths in app-port5001.js rather than within the canonical admin.js pipeline. While checking basic admin role status, they bypass origin restriction (restrictAdminOrigin), API key validation (validateAdminApiKey), fine-grained permission enforcement (requireAdminPermission), and tamper-proof audit logging (auditMiddleware). In addition, enrollments-admin.js leaks unmasked student PII across all users."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Several administrative routers are mounted directly on top-level paths in app-port5001.js rather than within the canonical admin.js pipeline. While checking basic admin role status, they bypass origin restriction (restrictAdminOrigin), API key validation (validateAdminApiKey), fine-grained permission enforcement (requireAdminPermission), and tamper-proof audit logging (auditMiddleware). In addition, enrollments-admin.js leaks unmasked student PII across all users.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/backend/src/app-port5001.js lines 1126, 1144, and 1147, administrative routers (enrollmentsAdminRoutes, stagesRoutes, fortspyRoutes) are mounted at root level with minimal middleware (protect, admin) instead of going through the complete admin pipeline configured in apps/backend/src/api/routes/admin.js.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nRetire enrollments-admin.js in favor of canonical admin-enrollments.js mounted inside admin.js. Move all standalone administrative endpoints (stages, fortspy, practice) inside admin.js."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high",
                "likelihood": "medium",
                "impact": "high",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-04-UNHANDLED-POOL-CONNECT-CRASH",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_04_UNHANDLED_POOL_CONNECT_CRASH",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Systemic Process Crash on DB Connection Pool Exhaustion across 113 Handlers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Across 113 route handlers in apps/backend/src/, database client acquisition via const client = await pool.connect(); is invoked outside the handler try/catch block. In Express 4, unhandled promise rejections in async route functions bubble to process.on('unhandledRejection'), which executes fatalShutdown and terminates the Node.js process via process.exit(1)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Across 113 route handlers in apps/backend/src/, database client acquisition via const client = await pool.connect(); is invoked outside the handler try/catch block. In Express 4, unhandled promise rejections in async route functions bubble to process.on('unhandledRejection'), which executes fatalShutdown and terminates the Node.js process via process.exit(1).\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn Express 4, async errors are not caught automatically unless wrapped by asyncHandler. When pool.connect() is called prior to try { ... }, any timeout, pool saturation, or database restart causes an unhandled rejection, triggering fatalShutdown in app-port5001.js line 1613.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nDeclare let client; and move client = await pool.connect(); inside the try block for all 113 identified handlers, ensuring client.release() is called in finally."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high",
                "likelihood": "high",
                "impact": "high",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-05-SESSION-CACHE-FAIL-OPEN",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_05_SESSION_CACHE_FAIL_OPEN",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Session Invalidation Bypass via Provisional Fail-Open Cache Injection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When a user session is revoked or deleted from the database, the authentication middleware fails open instead of failing closed, synthesizing a provisional active session cache entry with a 60-second TTL. An attacker retaining a compromised JWT can continue accessing the API for up to 60 seconds after explicit revocation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When a user session is revoked or deleted from the database, the authentication middleware fails open instead of failing closed, synthesizing a provisional active session cache entry with a 60-second TTL. An attacker retaining a compromised JWT can continue accessing the API for up to 60 seconds after explicit revocation.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/backend/src/middleware/auth.middleware.js lines 528-542, when sessionCheck.rows.length === 0, the code logs an alert but calls setCachedSession(decoded.sessionId, { isActive: true, provisional: true }, 60_000) and allows the request through.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nReplace the provisional cache injection in auth.middleware.js with an immediate 401 Unauthorized response."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium",
                "likelihood": "medium",
                "impact": "medium",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-06-AUDIT-LOG-URL-PII-LEAK",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_06_AUDIT_LOG_URL_PII_LEAK",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Sensitive URL Parameters and PII Leakage into Audit Logs Database Table"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The audit logging middleware redacts sensitive fields in JSON request bodies and query objects, but records req.originalUrl verbatim into details.path and request_path columns. Any tokens, reset keys, or student personal information transmitted in query strings are permanently stored in plaintext in the public.audit_logs table."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The audit logging middleware redacts sensitive fields in JSON request bodies and query objects, but records req.originalUrl verbatim into details.path and request_path columns. Any tokens, reset keys, or student personal information transmitted in query strings are permanently stored in plaintext in the public.audit_logs table.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/backend/src/middleware/audit.middleware.js lines 258-266, details.path is assigned req.originalUrl without stripping the query string component, bypassing the redaction logic applied to req.query.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nScrub query string parameters from req.originalUrl before recording in audit_logs."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium",
                "likelihood": "medium",
                "impact": "medium",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-07-CLIENT-IP-FORWARDED-SPOOF",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_07_CLIENT_IP_FORWARDED_SPOOF",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Client IP Spoofing Risk via Untrusted X-Forwarded-For Header Extraction"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Several backend handlers extract client IP addresses by reading raw req.headers['x-forwarded-for'] rather than utilizing Express's validated req.ip property. This allows clients to spoof IP addresses, potentially evading rate limiters or poisoning audit logs."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Several backend handlers extract client IP addresses by reading raw req.headers['x-forwarded-for'] rather than utilizing Express's validated req.ip property. This allows clients to spoof IP addresses, potentially evading rate limiters or poisoning audit logs.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/backend/src/api/routes/phoneAuth.js:61 and audit.middleware.js:54, custom code parses the raw X-Forwarded-For header directly instead of relying on Express's trust proxy configuration (app.set('trust proxy', 1)).\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nRefactor IP resolution helpers to use req.ip exclusively."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low",
                "likelihood": "medium",
                "impact": "low",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-08-CSRF-EXEMPT-ADMIN-PAYMENTS",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_08_CSRF_EXEMPT_ADMIN_PAYMENTS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Critical CSRF Bypass on Admin Payments & Sessions via Relative Path Matching"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The CSRF middleware defines exempt path prefixes using relative req.path matching rather than req.originalUrl. Because sub-routers mounted at root match relative paths, administrative endpoints for payment refunds, payment overrides, and session terminations are unintentionally exempted from CSRF token validation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The CSRF middleware defines exempt path prefixes using relative req.path matching rather than req.originalUrl. Because sub-routers mounted at root match relative paths, administrative endpoints for payment refunds, payment overrides, and session terminations are unintentionally exempted from CSRF token validation.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/backend/src/middleware/csrf.middleware.js lines 409-431, csrfExemptPaths contains '/payments' and '/sessions'. The check isExempt = csrfExemptPaths.some((p) => req.path.startsWith(p)) checks relative path, exempting all sub-routes mounted under those names.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nMatch exemptions strictly against req.originalUrl and limit payment exemptions strictly to webhook endpoints (/api/webhooks/razorpay)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "critical",
                "likelihood": "high",
                "impact": "critical",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-BE-09-PRO-PASS-CAMELCASE-REVOCATION",
              "name": "FINDING_BE_09_PRO_PASS_CAMELCASE_REVOCATION",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pro Pass Subscription Accidental Revocation via normalizeFields Destructuring"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The global normalizeFields middleware converts incoming request body keys to snake_case. However, admin-users.js destructures camelCase variables ({ isProUser, proPassExpiry, passType }), resulting in undefined variables. The endpoint evaluates isProUser: !!undefined (false) and pass_type: 'free', actively canceling student subscriptions when an administrator attempts to update or extend them."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The global normalizeFields middleware converts incoming request body keys to snake_case. However, admin-users.js destructures camelCase variables ({ isProUser, proPassExpiry, passType }), resulting in undefined variables. The endpoint evaluates isProUser: !!undefined (false) and pass_type: 'free', actively canceling student subscriptions when an administrator attempts to update or extend them.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/backend/src/api/routes/admin-users.js line 118, const { isProUser, proPassExpiry, passType } = req.body; fails to match the normalized keys is_pro_user, pro_pass_expiry, and pass_type generated by normalize-fields.js.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nUpdate destructuring in admin-users.js to check snake_case keys with camelCase fallbacks."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "critical",
                "likelihood": "high",
                "impact": "critical",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-CLIENT-01-ADMIN-MATHRENDERER-DOM-XSS",
              "name": "FINDING_CLIENT_01_ADMIN_MATHRENDERER_DOM_XSS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Stored DOM XSS via Sanitizer Ordering Inversion in MathRenderer Explanation Grid"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the admin panel MathRenderer component, sanitizeHtml is called BEFORE formatExplanationTables. Inside formatExplanationTables, header cell content is extracted via DOMParser .textContent, which unescapes HTML entities, and then assigned directly to title.innerHTML. This enables stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in the administrative dashboard whenever questions with crafted pedagogical tables are viewed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "In the admin panel MathRenderer component, sanitizeHtml is called BEFORE formatExplanationTables. Inside formatExplanationTables, header cell content is extracted via DOMParser .textContent, which unescapes HTML entities, and then assigned directly to title.innerHTML. This enables stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in the administrative dashboard whenever questions with crafted pedagogical tables are viewed.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/admin-panel/src/shared/components/MathRenderer.jsx lines 157-158, the order of sanitization is inverted compared to frontend. Calling sanitizeHtml before formatting allows formatExplanationTables to introduce unescaped HTML elements into title.innerHTML.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nInvert processing order so sanitizeHtml runs after formatExplanationTables, and assign header text via textContent."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high",
                "likelihood": "medium",
                "impact": "high",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-CLIENT-02-ADMIN-MULTITAB-SESSION-RACE",
              "name": "FINDING_CLIENT_02_ADMIN_MULTITAB_SESSION_RACE",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Multi-Tab Session Wipe Race Condition during Token Rotation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the admin panel apiClient, rotated tokens trigger localStorage.removeItem('trstprep_token'). Sibling browser tabs listening for storage events interpret the null newValue as an instruction to clear their own sessionStorage and memory token, immediately aborting active administrative workflows with 401 Unauthorized errors."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "In the admin panel apiClient, rotated tokens trigger localStorage.removeItem('trstprep_token'). Sibling browser tabs listening for storage events interpret the null newValue as an instruction to clear their own sessionStorage and memory token, immediately aborting active administrative workflows with 401 Unauthorized errors.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/admin-panel/src/shared/lib/apiClient.js lines 90-105, window.addEventListener('storage') checks if (!e.newValue && typeof sessionStorage !== 'undefined') sessionStorage.removeItem('trstprep_token'). Line 115 in persistRotatedTokens explicitly removes the localStorage key, inadvertently dispatching this eviction event to other tabs.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nRemove the sessionStorage.removeItem trigger from the storage event listener, or use BroadcastChannel with explicit message types (TOKEN_ROTATED vs LOGOUT)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium",
                "likelihood": "high",
                "impact": "medium",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-CLIENT-03-ADMIN-RBAC-PRIVILEGE-ESCALATION",
              "name": "FINDING_CLIENT_03_ADMIN_RBAC_PRIVILEGE_ESCALATION",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Administrative Privilege Escalation via Missing Sub-Role Checks in Role Management UI"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In RolePermissionsManager.jsx, permissions checks evaluate canManage = userPerms.includes('users:edit'). Any junior operator granted users:edit (e.g. for managing student profiles) can access the role configuration interface and grant themselves or other roles wildcard * permissions across all system modules."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "In RolePermissionsManager.jsx, permissions checks evaluate canManage = userPerms.includes('users:edit'). Any junior operator granted users:edit (e.g. for managing student profiles) can access the role configuration interface and grant themselves or other roles wildcard * permissions across all system modules.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/admin-panel/src/features/admin/users-enrollments/RolePermissionsManager.jsx lines 71-76, canManage permits role editing if userPerms includes users:edit. Backend admin-roles.js also lacks validation preventing callers from assigning permissions greater than their own.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nRequire isSuper or wildcard * permission to access RolePermissionsManager and update backend admin-roles.js to enforce permission subsets."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high",
                "likelihood": "medium",
                "impact": "high",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-CLIENT-04-ADMIN-PDF-VIEWER-IFRAME-INJECTION",
              "name": "FINDING_CLIENT_04_ADMIN_PDF_VIEWER_IFRAME_INJECTION",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Arbitrary Origin Iframe Embedding & Unrestricted Blob Execution in Admin PDF Viewer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The PDFViewer component allows embedding arbitrary http:, https:, and blob: URLs inside an unsandboxed iframe within the Admin Panel. An attacker capable of submitting external URLs in study materials or syllabus links can embed third-party web pages or execute arbitrary HTML/JS via blob: URIs."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The PDFViewer component allows embedding arbitrary http:, https:, and blob: URLs inside an unsandboxed iframe within the Admin Panel. An attacker capable of submitting external URLs in study materials or syllabus links can embed third-party web pages or execute arbitrary HTML/JS via blob: URIs.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/admin-panel/src/shared/components/common/PDFViewer.jsx lines 5-20 and 136-143, isSafeUrl allows any http, https, or blob protocol without restricting hostnames to trusted storage origins, and the iframe element lacks a sandbox attribute.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nAdd sandbox attribute to the iframe and restrict allowed URLs to trusted storage origins."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium",
                "likelihood": "medium",
                "impact": "medium",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-CLIENT-05-FE-PROFILE-BANNER-CSS-INJECTION",
              "name": "FINDING_CLIENT_05_FE_PROFILE_BANNER_CSS_INJECTION",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Potential CSS Exfiltration and SSRF via Unsanitized user.banner in Profile Header"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The ProfileHeader component interpolates user.banner directly into an inline backgroundImage CSS property without validating URL structure or protocol. An attacker supplying a crafted banner URL can cause broken style attributes or trigger unauthorized outbound tracking requests."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The ProfileHeader component interpolates user.banner directly into an inline backgroundImage CSS property without validating URL structure or protocol. An attacker supplying a crafted banner URL can cause broken style attributes or trigger unauthorized outbound tracking requests.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/frontend/src/pages/dashboard/profile/ProfileHeader.jsx line 52, style={{ backgroundImage: `url(${user.banner})` }} lacks protocol and character sanitization.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nSanitize user.banner using isSafeImageUrl and encodeURI before setting style."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low",
                "likelihood": "low",
                "impact": "low",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-CLIENT-06-FE-LEADERBOARD-EXPORT-HTML-INJECTION",
              "name": "FINDING_CLIENT_06_FE_LEADERBOARD_EXPORT_HTML_INJECTION",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Export PDF Window HTML Injection via Unescaped Filename Prop in Leaderboard"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In the Leaderboard component's ExportMenu, the filename prop is injected unescaped into title and h2 tags inside printWin.document.write(). While current callers pass static strings, dynamic caller values could break the HTML structure and execute scripts."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "In the Leaderboard component's ExportMenu, the filename prop is injected unescaped into title and h2 tags inside printWin.document.write(). While current callers pass static strings, dynamic caller values could break the HTML structure and execute scripts.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/frontend/src/pages/tests/Leaderboard.jsx line 583, helper esc(c) is used for table columns and rows, but filename is interpolated directly into raw HTML without escaping.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nWrap filename with esc(filename) inside printWin.document.write template."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low",
                "likelihood": "low",
                "impact": "low",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-CLIENT-07-ADMIN-UNBOUNDED-FILE-UPLOAD",
              "name": "FINDING_CLIENT_07_ADMIN_UNBOUNDED_FILE_UPLOAD",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unbounded File Uploads without Client-Side Pre-flight Size & MIME Checks in Study Materials"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The ContentManagement component in the admin panel uploads files directly to /admin/assets/upload without client-side size or MIME type pre-flight checks. Attempting to upload very large files causes browser unresponsiveness, network buffer exhaustion, or gateway timeouts."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The ContentManagement component in the admin panel uploads files directly to /admin/assets/upload without client-side size or MIME type pre-flight checks. Attempting to upload very large files causes browser unresponsiveness, network buffer exhaustion, or gateway timeouts.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/admin-panel/src/features/admin/study-materials/ContentManagement.jsx lines 108-128, handleFileUpload immediately appends raw file objects into FormData and triggers an API post without pre-flight validation.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nAdd pre-flight file size checks (e.g. <= 50MB for documents) before constructing FormData."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low",
                "likelihood": "medium",
                "impact": "low",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-CLIENT-08-ADMIN-SW-AUTOUPDATE-RELOAD",
              "name": "FINDING_CLIENT_08_ADMIN_SW_AUTOUPDATE_RELOAD",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unprompted Mid-Session Reload via Service Worker autoUpdate in Admin Dashboard"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Vite PWA plugin in the admin panel is configured with registerType: 'autoUpdate'. When a new build is deployed, the service worker client automatically invokes window.location.reload() upon activation, destroying active form state and unsubmitted administrative modifications."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Vite PWA plugin in the admin panel is configured with registerType: 'autoUpdate'. When a new build is deployed, the service worker client automatically invokes window.location.reload() upon activation, destroying active form state and unsubmitted administrative modifications.\n\nRoot Cause:\nIn apps/admin-panel/vite.config.js line 41, registerType is set to 'autoUpdate'. In frontend vite.config.js, this was intentionally changed to 'prompt' to prevent unexpected reloads.\n\nRemediation Strategy:\nChange registerType to 'prompt' in apps/admin-panel/vite.config.js."
              },
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low",
                "likelihood": "high",
                "impact": "low",
                "confidence": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "FINDING-DB-01-RLS-POLQUAL-NULL-BYPASS",
              "name": "FINDING_DB_01_RLS_POLQUAL_NULL_BYPASS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Active Row Level Security Policy Bypass via current_user_id_setting() IS NULL"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Across 43 database tables in PostgreSQL, Row Level Security policies evaluate expressions containing OR (current_user_id_setting() IS NULL). Any database query executed without an explicit session user ID GUC evaluates this condition to TRUE, exposing private multi-tenant records across attempts, two-factor secrets, and payments."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
